  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how the panels render. The rule's status is still \"pending\" because it hadn't run yet when I created it.\n\n**Data:** The sample logs hold web requests with these fields:\n- `timestamp`\n- `response` (the HTTP code, stored as text)\n- `bytes`\n- `extension`\n- `clientip`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os`\n- `url`\n- `agent`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-0d2797ea Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- Total requests, as a single number.\n- Requests over time, as a line chart.\n- Requests by response code, as a pie chart.\n- Average bytes by file extension, as a bar chart.\n\nIt is at `http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-0d2797ea-web-logs-overview-4d833341961b`.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-0d2797ea 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute. It counts documents whose `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]` over the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is above 10. The rule has no notification action attached, so it will fire but won't notify anyone.\n\nThe sample data is static, so with a 5-minute window the rule will probably find no recent matches unless you load newer logs.",
